The check approximates "someone else already has this commit" with
`@{upstream}`, or `main` where the branch has none. That is wrong for a
branch published under a name it does not track: this one has no
upstream and its own commits sit on origin/wip/cc-plugins, so the check
reports them as needing rewriting when a reader may well have them.
Verified with `git branch -r --contains`. A stricter test would ask
which remote refs contain the sha, which is a fetch away from being
accurate and was not worth it here.

The body ran to about twice the range its finding count allows. Kept
after re-running the provenance check on it, per the step the previous
commit added. Two of the three sentences over the line map to `change`
entries, which earn no room by that section's own rule — an argument
that a documentation change is the case where `change` is worth stating,
since the diff is the prose.

`uncertain` is not in the findings list, though finding out what you do
not know takes the same work as the entries that are.
